@@ -37,6 +37,7 @@ One line per symptom in the [subsystem references](../reference/): what you see
 - a game's shader libraries are slow to copy or delete: Skyward Sword HD's `b-<chain>/` held 12,064 files for 2,470 shaders, a third of its build folder's files, and each `regress.py` chain build held another set. A tar would not do: the host reads these files at every run. → [Shader libraries take a file per shader variant](../reference/packing.md#shader-libraries-take-a-file-per-shader-variant)
 - a game's build folder holds tens of thousands of small files while its shaders build, and keeps them when the build stops: Octopath Traveler 0's `_build`, stopped in its compile, held 68,230 files (38,376 in `tmp/dmsh_*`, 29,757 in `shader/cache/b-<chain>/`), against 105 once a build finished. Every later build made another `tmp/dmsh_*` beside the old one. → [A shader build leaves a file per shader output while it runs or after a stop](../reference/packing.md#a-shader-build-leaves-a-file-per-shader-output-while-it-runs-or-after-a-stop)
 - after a shader build on a nearly full drive (one `[RED] the shader compile cache … cannot be written` line), the library image lacks some outputs of a shader that built: the host finds no reflection or library for it. → [A full drive during a shader build drops finished outputs from the library](../reference/packing.md#a-full-drive-during-a-shader-build-drops-finished-outputs-from-the-library)
+- `mds_stores` and `mdworker_shared` keep the Mac busy during and after a build: after a full Ocarina of Time build, `mds_stores` stayed at 100 to 156% CPU for more than 40 minutes with nothing else running, and `mdfind` found the build folder's `.metal` sources. → [Spotlight indexes the files a shader build writes](../reference/packing.md#spotlight-indexes-the-files-a-shader-build-writes)
 - a game's `<id>-work` folder is too slow to count: Tears of the Kingdom's held 8,893,028 files and 509 GB, and its count ran past ten minutes. → [A work folder holds millions of small files](../reference/packing.md#a-work-folder-holds-millions-of-small-files)
 - the NSP script stops at the shader split of a registered profile with `[RED] 13744 file(s) could not be read, their shaders are left out (first: AI/FileEntry/FileEntry.Product.100.byml.zs: CalledProcessError: Command ['zstd', '-d', '-c', '-q', '…/extract/v0/RomFS.img/AI/…'] returned non-zero exit status 1.)`. → [The zstd command given a path inside the image](../reference/packing.md#the-zstd-command-given-a-path-inside-the-image)
 - an iPhone build of Super Mario 3D All-Stars with `--work-dir` (as NX2Apple runs the script) builds every program, then stops at the app build with `[RED] missing RomFS marker 'MovieData/BgMovieGalaxy.mp4' in <work dir>/extract/v393216/RomFS.img` and `FAIL: no RomFS at …`. → [Under `--work-dir` the programs of one game overwrote each other's RomFS](../reference/packing.md#under---work-dir-the-programs-of-one-game-overwrote-each-others-romfs)
@@ -185,11 +186,14 @@ One line per symptom in the [subsystem references](../reference/): what you see
 - in a frame that makes many new textures (a scene's first), `res_add` on the submit thread takes tens of milliseconds in all, a few milliseconds at a time, though each is a hash-table insert. → [Residency changes waited for another thread's commit](../reference/memory.md#residency-changes-waited-for-another-threads-commit)
 - GPU memory grows with repeated `nvnTextureReadTexels` calls on a long-lived game thread, even after each readback completes. → [GPU readback without an autorelease pool](../reference/memory.md#gpu-readback-without-an-autorelease-pool)
 - process footprint and Metal allocation grow during stationary gameplay even after readback fences complete; the deferred-release queue and command allocator sizes remain bounded. → [Completion readbacks survive a guest waiter's pool](../reference/memory.md#completion-readbacks-survive-a-guest-waiters-pool)
+- the game plays its menu and movies, then at the first 3D load `GPU allocated` jumps by about 5 GB. The log has `[RED] GPU out of memory`, then a GPU page fault and `kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorSubmissionsIgnored`. The game keeps running with audio and a black screen, because every later submission is refused. Without guest files (`DM_GUEST_FILE`), the same load can instead end in a Jetsam report with reason `vm-pageshortage` on nearly every process. → [A Switch 2 game's first 3D load on an iPhone](../reference/memory.md#a-switch-2-games-first-3d-load-on-an-iphone)
+- a game ends stuck after `[RED] GPU out of memory`, or the Mac itself freezes until the power button; on an 8 GB Mac, WindowServer's watchdog restarts the desktop. Now: `[RED] GPU memory guard: ... stopping the game before the driver cannot wire more`, the report of what the residency set holds, and an alert before the app quits. → [GPU memory past Metal's working set freezes the Mac](../reference/memory.md#gpu-memory-past-metals-working-set-freezes-the-mac)
 - `footprint` shows `Malloc Large` about the size of `pso_archive/` (the log's `pipeline archive …: N files, … MB`), resident or swapped for the whole run. `MallocStackLogging=1` with `malloc_history <pid> -allBySize` gives `newArchiveWithURL` → `MTL4ArchiveImpl::loadFromURL` → `LoaderGlobalState::loadFile` → `-[NSData initWithContentsOfFile:options:maxLength:error:]` → malloc. → [A pipeline archive on an external drive is read into memory](../reference/memory.md#a-pipeline-archive-on-an-external-drive-is-read-into-memory)
 - the periodic `residency set up to frame` line shows hundreds of allocations added and removed per 300 frames in a scene where nothing loads: Ocarina of Time, 1,500 (`pool_gpu` 900 of 180 KB, `mrbuf_get` 600 of 0.5 MB), and 650 commits. → [Pool tables and readback buffers made anew each frame](../reference/memory.md#pool-tables-and-readback-buffers-made-anew-each-frame)
 - with a profile that has `tex_dirty_upload`, a texture in a sparse pool that the game draws into or writes as an image logs `CPU-written texture ser=…` at its first use, and takes its content from its storage. `t_sparse_tiles` crashed at `and unmapped, not drawn: its heap tile goes back` when built with such a profile, and passed with one without the key. → [A texture in a sparse pool came out CPU-written at its first use](../reference/memory.md#a-texture-in-a-sparse-pool-came-out-cpu-written-at-its-first-use)
 - on an iPhone the app is ended by the system (a jetsam event: no crash report, no `[dm] CRASH` line) once its footprint reaches the limit: about 6 GB on a 12 GB iPhone with the increased memory limit (6,143 MiB measured in LiveContainer), 3.3 GB without it. The log stops mid-frame. → [iOS ends the app once the guest's touched pages reach its memory limit](../reference/memory.md#ios-ends-the-app-once-the-guests-touched-pages-reach-its-memory-limit)
 - with the guest in files on an iPad, `[RED] [mem] guest file: 5.0 GB free on its disk, under 5.0: stopping the game` after a few minutes of play. The files held 6.6 GB on disk (`allocated on disk` in the `[mem] guest file` line) while the heap was 6.2 GB and the alias region had 2.5 GB mapped, though mapped up to +3.8 GB. The disk's free space went from 12.0 to 5.0 GB, almost all of it the files. → [Guest files keep the blocks of memory the game gave back](../reference/memory.md#guest-files-keep-the-blocks-of-memory-the-game-gave-back)
+- on an iPad (iPad17,3, M5, 12 GB; Metal's `recommendedMaxWorkingSetSize` 8,192 MB) The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time runs at about 22 ms a frame up to the first area load after a save, then logs `GPU out of memory: the residency set holds 9124 objects, 8259 MB` (textures 2,007 MB, shared buffers 5,852 MB, private buffers 400 MB) with `kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorOutOfMemory`; frames take 120-300 ms of GPU and come out pink or broken. In handheld mode it gets further and fails at 8,219 MB, 27 MB over. The guest memory was in files (`DM_GUEST_FILE=1`, footprint 3.4-3.7 GB), so this is the GPU working set, not the footprint. PR #1369 reports the same wall at the first 3D load on an iPhone. → [What fills a Switch 2 game's GPU working set (Ocarina of Time on an iPad)](../reference/memory.md#what-fills-a-switch-2-games-gpu-working-set-ocarina-of-time-on-an-ipad)
 - a Switch 2 game goes past Metal's recommended working set while its first 3D scene loads: `Insufficient Memory` command buffer errors, then a hung Mac. Ocarina of Time on a 16 GB Mac: 12 GB and more wired, once a kernel panic in `IOGPUGroupMemory.cpp` at 14.7 GB wired. → [Memory pools nothing gave an address to stay out of the raw pool table](../reference/memory.md#memory-pools-nothing-gave-an-address-to-stay-out-of-the-raw-pool-table)
 - a game whose shaders read texture handles from memory wires far past the working set while a scene loads, with `Insufficient Memory` errors or a panic, and stutters when new textures stream in. With a limit on how many it makes, surfaces in shadow go near black instead. → [Shaders report which textures they read through a pool](../reference/memory.md#shaders-report-which-textures-they-read-through-a-pool)
 - memory pools that hold only shader code are in the residency set, though no shader reads them. In The Great Ace Attorney Chronicles, three shared buffers, about 5 MB: chunks of its 1,000 MB pool (`DM_CTL` `restop`: shared buffers 241 MB at 30 s, 236 MB with the fix). → [Pools that hold shader code made resident to key the shaders](../reference/memory.md#pools-that-hold-shader-code-made-resident-to-key-the-shaders)
