+### A GPU-only pool's render targets share its memory
+
+- **Symptom:** GPU memory far above what the game's render target pools hold: on a 16 GB Mac (Metal's working set about 10.7 GB) or an iPad the residency set reaches the working set, frames take 100 ms and more, and command buffers fail or the submissions block in the kernel. `restop` shows thousands of render targets, many over the same pool offsets under other descriptions.
+- **Cause:** engines place their transient render targets over each other in a `CPU_NO_ACCESS` pool, as a frame graph does: NVN gives them that pool's memory, whatever their number. The host made a private Metal texture for each `(pool, offset, description)`, so every description took memory of its own. In Ocarina of Time (Switch 2) the 305 MB render target pool held 888 shared textures, 2,412 MB as Metal textures, and its `CPU_NO_ACCESS` pools 3,001 MB of textures in 804 MB of pools. The resolution families of [the entry above](#live-resolution-variants-share-physical-image-storage) are a narrower case: one offset, one format, `COMPRESSIBLE|IMAGE`.
+- **Fix:** `pool_heap_texture` in `nvn_mtl.m` gives each `CPU_NO_ACCESS` pool (up to 1 GB) one placement heap, and places each of its render targets (`COMPRESSIBLE`, not sparse, linear or display) at its pool offset scaled by 1.25, since a Metal texture can be a little larger than NVN's layout of it. Ranges apart in the pool are apart in the heap and ranges that overlap there may overlap here, so memory is shared as on NVN and never between textures NVN keeps apart; a texture that does not fit its scaled range keeps an allocation of its own. Heap memory no texture has covered yet takes the texture's initial content (`tex_init_fill`, or zeros); over memory another texture has covered, a new texture inherits what is there and nothing is written, as on NVN: writing initial content there overwrote the live textures sharing it. The heap goes with the pool (`mtl_alias_drop_pool`) once its last texture does. NXForge 0.22.0 turns it off by default (`DM_NVN_POOL_HEAP=1` turns it on): nxnative found that a heap mirroring the pool corrupts the picture while a game's dynamic resolution moves, and recycles render-target heaps instead ([the entry above](#render-targets-that-move-with-the-dynamic-resolution-get-new-metal-memory-each-time)).
+- **Limits:** a texture without `COMPRESSIBLE` (data a game reads into a `CPU_NO_ACCESS` pool, which `tex_init_fill` takes from its storage each time the texture is made) and pools the CPU can write keep one allocation per description. A pool over 1 GB is left out, as its heap would be allocated whole.
+- **Check:** `make -C dm/gfx/tests GAME=<id> check_t_pool_heap` runs under Metal validation with direct and deferred encoding and with the switch off: descriptions over one storage share the heap, storage apart stays apart, a texture made over used memory leaves another's pixels as they are, the other kinds stay apart, and the heap leaves the residency set with the pool. `restop` (DM_CTL) prints the heaps made, their size, and the render targets placed and kept apart.
+- **Found on:** The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time (Switch 2, `0400486024DEC000` v131072, profile in [#1299](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/1299)) on a 24 GB M5 Pro, Kokiri Forest and the Great Deku Tree: 62 pool heaps of 671 MB in all for its `CPU_NO_ACCESS` render targets; the residency set went from 8 to 10 GB to 6 to 7 GB, inside a 16 GB Mac's working set (checked with that budget imposed), with no visual change over repeated scene changes.
 
