+### Samples that do not overlap on Apple's M1: the border branch and the coordinate NaN select
+
+- **Symptom:** Mario Kart World on an M1 Pro (16 GB) took 27 to 28 ms of GPU time a frame at the title, three times the 8.3 to 9.3 ms of a 16 GB M6, more than the difference in throughput. Its cube prefilter (fragment `4744589bb8f9f7dd`, 6 faces x 32 cube samples a pixel at explicit LOD) took 2.2 ms a chain, three chains a frame.
+- **Cause:** on the M1's GPU compiler (`applegpu_g13s`) two things the build puts around a sample each keep a shader's samples from overlapping: the per-sample uniform branch of [Border colours Metal has no sampler state for](rendering.md#border-colours-metal-has-no-sampler-state-for) (`dm_bs`), and the translator's guard on float texture coordinates, `select(c, 0, isnan(c))` (vendored Ryujinx `Instructions.cs`, its only Select of an IsNan; 183,710 of them in Mario Kart World's shaders). The prefilter's chain in a Metal harness, uniforms from the game: 2,180 us as built, 2,135 without the select, 3,443 without the branch, 879 without both. `-O2` or `-fmetal-math-mode=relaxed` change nothing. On a cube direction any operation costs as much as the select: `fmax`, `fmin`, `precise::fmax`, per component or on the vector took the cube-array prefilter `765844f5609bb11b` from 810 to 4,216 to 4,257 us a chain, while a guard on its layer only stayed at 803.
+- **Fix:** two profile keys, shader inputs both (`dmsh_build.py`). `shader_border_emu 0` leaves out the twin samplers: a sampler with a border colour Metal lacks keeps the nearest Metal one, so it is only for a game that never prints the host's `which Metal lacks` line. `shader_nan_coord fmax` writes the guard as `fmax(c, -FLT_MAX)` (`nan_coord_fmax`): Apple's `max` returns the operand that is not NaN, in fast math too (one `fmax` per sample is left in the IR), so a NaN coordinate reads the same texel as with the select through clamp-to-edge, repeat and mirrored repeat, and the whole border colour instead of half of it through clamp-to-border. A cube direction keeps no guard and a cube array keeps it on its layer: `fmax` would not keep the select's face either (a NaN axis picks the negative one). An exact form, `fmax(c, 0) + fmin(c, 0)`, is as slow as the select (5,546 us a chain).
+- **Found on:** [Mario Kart World](../../dm/games/0400C3F00006E000/README.md#shaders): with both keys the title's GPU time went from 27.0 to 27.8 ms to 18.3 to 19.5 ms a frame (alternating runs) and the first race from 39.9 to 47.7 fps; the prefilter chains from 2.2 to 0.67 ms. The main 2048x1152 scene passes got faster too (791 to 416 us a pass), so it is not only the cube shader.
+- **Check:** without a guard a NaN coordinate through clamp-to-edge read the far edge on the M1, not texel 0, so the guard is needed. `test_dmsh_nan_coord.py` checks the rewrite on 2D, cube and cube-array samples. Not measured on later Apple GPUs, where the default stays as it was.
